  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...Increased humidity and warmer temperatures \ncontinue tomorrow.\n\nWarmer temperatures with increased humidity continue into tomorrow \nas an anomalous moisture plume and higher 925 mb temperatures advect \ninto southern New England via SW flow. Looking ahead into tonight, \nthe increased moisture and winds dropping off in the interior may \nfavor the development of some patchy fog, especially in the CT \nValley. With 925 mb temperatures reaching around 16-19C in the \nafternoon Thursday, high temperatures in the low 80s are a \npossibility, especially in the river valleys. These higher low level \ntemperatures remain over the region overnight as well with high \npressure affixed just to our south continuing SW flow; lows tonight \nwill likely only drop into 60s as opposed to the areas of 50s and \n40s we've seen the past few nights. PWATs up to 2.0\" and dewpoints \nin the upper 60s will make it feel more humid, too. Despite the \nincreased humidity, the region should remain dry for much of the day \ntomorrow.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...Scattered showers possible Thursday evening \ninto Friday morning. Dry during the day Friday.\n\nA weak shortwave trough progresses eastward behind the plume of \nmoisture, which will aid in the development of scattered showers \nacross southern New England. As previously mentioned, latest \nguidance has shown PWATs reaching ans even exceeding 2.0\" Thursday \nafternoon/evening. However, we are not anticipating widespread \ndownpours as guidance continues to track the stronger forcing across \nnorthern parts of New England. Global models and a number of the \nCAMs (with the exception of the NAM) are in agreement that higher \nrainfall totals should generally remain to our north, and ensemble \nprobabilities also favor this outcome. Totals across southern New \nEngland should primarily range between 0.1\" to 0.3\" based on the \nmore northern track of the wave. The latest CAMs have still shown \nsome elevated instability Thursday night, so a low chance of some \nisolated downpours cannot be completely ruled out. Totals could also \nincrease slightly if the wave were to end up tracking a little \nfurther south, but the general consensus is that the greatest \nforcing will remain to our north and we could generally expect a few \ntenths of an inch.\n\nFollowing this system, drier air pushes back in with NW flow, which \nwill aid in clearing skies through the day and bringing temperatures \nback to a more seasonable range. \n\nKEY MESSAGE 3... Rain chances return some time late Saturday night \ninto Monday morning. There remains uncertainty in the details such \nas coverage, timing, amounts. \n\nA high pressure should move across northern New England Saturday, \nthen over the North Atlantic Sunday. This should clear the path for \na low pressure to move from the lower Great Lakes south of New \nEngland late some time late Saturday night into Monday. This will \nlikely be our next period of rainfall. However, the timing is not \nyet finalized, especially with a high pressure over the ocean. Would \nnot be surprised if a slower timing develops.\n\nThere is also a question about how much rainfall we can expect \nacross our region. High pressure over eastern Canada may nudge far \nenough south to pus the ultimate storm track far enough south where \nwe could wind up dry Tuesday into Wednesday. Assuming this occurs, \ntotal rainfall amounts could be limited to a general 1-2 inches by \nMonday afternoon. Otherwise, with a nearly stationary front close to \nthe south coast of New England could trigger some showers right into \nmid week.",
